docker run --rm --network railway-dev-network -v D:/HOCTAP/HTPT:/workspace -w /workspace maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-25 mvn -B -ntp -pl backend-node -am "-Dtest=vn.nhom6.railway.raft.replication.FivePeerReplicationSafetyTest" "-Dsurefire.failIfNoSpecifiedTests=false" test

Result: PASS; Maven exit code 0.
FivePeerReplicationSafetyTest: 12 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors, 0 skipped.
The same class passed 12/0/0/0 in the original targeted run made at commit
e500296. The re-run above was performed at HEAD 7047b07; the test file is
byte-identical between e500296 and 7047b07 (git diff for that path is empty),
so the result applies to the D15 code commit.

Test methods (12):
1. replicatesFiveEntriesToAllFivePeersAndLogsConverge
2. logMatchingPropertyHoldsForEveryPairOfNodes
3. delayedFollowerCatchesUpInBoundedBatches
4. uncommittedConflictingSuffixConvergesWithoutDeletingMatchingPrefix
5. committedConflictFailsClosedAndKeepsLog
6. partitionedFollowersDoNotBlockProgressAndCatchUpAfterHeal
7. higherTermReplyStepsLeaderDown
8. lateHigherTermIsHandledButLateEqualTermIsIgnored
9. heartbeatPreservesFollowerLogAndDoesNotInflateMatchIndex
10. transportTimeoutInvalidResponseAndPeerErrorNeverCountAsAck
11. enforcesOneInFlightAppendPerPeerWithIndependentProgress
12. replicationNeverAdvancesLeaderCommitAndFollowersNeverOvershootTheirOwnLog

Scope of the timeout/error case:
- Case 10 drives RaftReplicationService with a fake RPC client whose future
  completes exceptionally (simulated timeout), returns a malformed response,
  or returns a peer error. This is a service-level check. Wire-level late
  replies in RaftTransportAdapter are covered by the pre-existing
  RaftTransportAdapterTest, because RaftTransportAdapter.TimeoutScheduler is
  package-private to vn.nhom6.railway.raft.core and cannot be driven from
  this test package.

Honest scope boundary:
- All D15 validation is FakeNetwork only. It does not prove real TCP framing,
  five Docker nodes on five hosts, OS-level socket timeouts, PostgreSQL
  durability, or recovery after process restart.
- Tests do not use Thread.sleep or real timers; scheduler rounds are stepped
  explicitly, so results are deterministic and fast (~2 s for the class).

